-- A+ Core 1 (220-1101) Domain 5 expansion, part B: objectives 5.4-5.7.
-- Tops up 5.4 (video/projector/display), 5.5 (mobile device issues),
-- 5.6 (printer issues) and 5.7 (wired/wireless network issues) to 33 items each.
-- Adds questions a1-q-900..a1-q-950 (51) and flashcards a1-f-900..a1-f-962 (63).
-- Symptom-driven throughout, matching the troubleshooting style of the domain.

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values

-- ===== 5.4 Video, projector and display issues (14) =====

('a1-q-900','aplus1',5,'5.4','mcq',1,$q$A conference-room monitor shows "No Signal - check cable" even though the connected laptop lists it as an extended display in Windows and the laptop's HDMI cable is firmly seated in the monitor's HDMI 2 port. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["The monitor's input source is still set to a different input, such as HDMI 1 or DisplayPort","The monitor's backlight has failed","The laptop's graphics driver is corrupted","The monitor has developed a cluster of dead pixels"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Windows already sees and enumerates the panel, so the video link and driver are working - the monitor is simply rendering a different input. Cycle the source button to HDMI 2. A failed backlight is the tempting pick, but a dead backlight produces a very dim image rather than an on-screen "No Signal" message, and that message is generated by the monitor's own electronics, proving the panel and its controller are alive.$q$),

('a1-q-901','aplus1',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$A user connects a 4K monitor at 60 Hz through a 25 ft HDMI cable and reports random sparkles, brief black flashes, and momentary signal dropouts. Setting the resolution down to 1920x1080 makes the problem disappear entirely. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["The cable does not have the bandwidth for a 4K 60 Hz signal over that length","The monitor panel is failing and needs replacement","The GPU is overheating and throttling","The monitor's colour profile is mismatched"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The fault tracks the required bandwidth, not the hardware: 4K at 60 Hz needs roughly 18 Gbps, and a long or uncertified passive HDMI cable cannot sustain it, so the link intermittently loses sync. Use a certified Premium High Speed or active/optical cable. A failing panel is the tempting answer, but a failing panel would misbehave at every resolution, not only above a specific bandwidth threshold.$q$),

('a1-q-902','aplus1',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$A ceiling-mounted projector powers on and its cooling fan runs, but nothing is projected and the lamp indicator glows solid red. The lamp-hour counter reads 4,120 hours against a rated lamp life of 4,000 hours. What should the technician do?$q$,$q$["Replace the projector lamp module and reset the lamp-hour counter","Change the input source on the projector","Replace the video cable between the source and the projector","Clean the air filter and restart the projector"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A red lamp LED combined with a lamp-hour count past the rated life is a burned-out bulb; replace the lamp assembly and reset the counter so the next end-of-life warning is accurate. Handle the new lamp by its housing only - skin oils on the glass create hot spots and cause premature failure. Changing the input is the tempting answer, but a wrong source produces a blue or "no signal" screen from a working lamp, not a dark screen with a lamp fault light.$q$),

('a1-q-903','aplus1',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$Text on a new 27-inch flat-panel monitor looks soft and slightly fuzzy at every position on the screen, though the image is bright and correctly coloured. The panel is native 2560x1440 and Windows is currently driving it at 1920x1080. What is the best fix?$q$,$q$["Set the display resolution to the panel's native 2560x1440","Increase the monitor's sharpness setting to maximum","Replace the video cable with a shorter one","Update the monitor's colour profile"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A flat panel has a fixed physical pixel grid, so any non-native resolution must be interpolated across mismatched pixels, which softens edges uniformly. Setting the native resolution restores a one-to-one pixel mapping. Raising the sharpness control is the tempting shortcut, but it only adds edge contrast to an already-scaled image and cannot recover the detail lost in scaling.$q$),

('a1-q-904','aplus1',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$A lobby OLED display has shown the same dashboard, with a fixed header bar and static side panel, 24 hours a day for a year. A faint permanent outline of that header is now visible even when the screen shows other content. What has occurred, and what mitigates it going forward?$q$,$q$["Burn-in from prolonged static content; rotate the content, enable pixel shift, and lower brightness","A cluster of stuck pixels; run a pixel-cycling utility overnight","Backlight bleed; replace the diffuser layer","A corrupted display driver; reinstall the graphics driver"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$OLED subpixels emit their own light and age with use, so pixels driven hard by unchanging content dim permanently relative to their neighbours, leaving a ghost of the static element. Mitigation is preventive: rotating content, screen savers, pixel-shift features, and reduced brightness. Stuck pixels are the tempting answer, but they are isolated dots locked to one colour from a transistor fault, not a faint reproduction of previously displayed imagery.$q$),

('a1-q-905','aplus1',5,'5.4','mcq',1,$q$A user reports one tiny dot near the centre of their laptop screen that stays black on every screen, including the BIOS setup screen and a full-white test image. What is this, and what is the appropriate response?$q$,$q$["A dead pixel; check the warranty against the manufacturer's dead-pixel policy","A stuck pixel; massage the area and run a colour-cycling utility","Dust behind the panel; disassemble and clean the display","Screen burn-in; display alternating content to reverse it"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A pixel that is permanently black on all content, including pre-boot screens, has a failed transistor and cannot be revived in software - the remedy is a warranty claim, and most vendors require a minimum number of defects before replacing a panel. A stuck pixel is the tempting alternative, but a stuck pixel is permanently lit in one colour (red, green, or blue) rather than black, and it is the stuck variety that sometimes responds to a pixel-cycling utility.$q$),

('a1-q-906','aplus1',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$After a user connects a new monitor, they report that the screen visibly flickers and motion looks choppy. Display settings show the refresh rate at 30 Hz although the monitor supports 60 Hz. What should the technician do first?$q$,$q$["Set the refresh rate to the monitor's supported 60 Hz and verify the cable and port support that mode","Reduce the monitor's brightness to stop the flicker","Replace the monitor under warranty","Disable hardware acceleration in the browser"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A 30 Hz refresh rate is low enough for the display's redraw to be perceptible as flicker and for motion to look choppy; raising it to the panel's supported 60 Hz resolves both, though the cable and port must have enough bandwidth to carry that mode at the chosen resolution. Replacing the monitor is the tempting escalation, but the panel is behaving exactly as configured, so no hardware has failed.$q$),

('a1-q-907','aplus1',5,'5.4','mcq',3,$q$One of two identical monitors on a workstation shows everything with a strong green tint. The tint is present on the BIOS/POST screen before Windows loads, and it follows the monitor when the two video cables are swapped between the ports. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["A damaged video cable or a bent/broken connector pin dropping a colour channel","An incorrect ICC colour profile assigned to that monitor in Windows","A corrupted graphics driver affecting only one output","The monitor's factory colour temperature preset is set to Cool"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Two clues make this physical: the tint appears at POST, before any operating system, driver, or colour profile is loaded, and it travels with the cable rather than with the port. A missing red or blue channel from a broken conductor or bent pin - classic on VGA and DVI connectors - produces exactly this cast. The ICC colour profile is the tempting answer, but a profile is applied by the OS and could not possibly tint the pre-boot POST screen.$q$),

('a1-q-908','aplus1',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$A presenter connects a laptop to a projector with a single HDMI cable. Video appears on the screen correctly, but the projector's speakers produce no sound, while the laptop's own speakers still play audio normally. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["The operating system's default playback device is still set to the laptop's internal speakers","HDMI carries video only, so a separate audio cable is required","The laptop's audio driver has failed","The projector lamp is at end of life"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$HDMI carries audio and video together, but the OS treats the projector as an additional playback device and does not always switch to it automatically - select the projector as the default output in sound settings. The idea that HDMI needs a separate audio cable is the tempting misconception (true for VGA and DVI-D, which are video only), but HDMI and DisplayPort both carry embedded audio.$q$),

('a1-q-909','aplus1',5,'5.4','multi',2,$q$A laptop screen appears almost black. Select the TWO findings that together indicate a failed backlight or inverter rather than a failed GPU or system board.$q$,$q$["The image is faintly visible on the panel when a bright flashlight is shone on it at an angle","An external monitor connected to the laptop displays the desktop normally","The laptop emits a repeating POST beep code at power-on","The screen shows brightly coloured garbled blocks and random artifacts","The laptop does not power on at all"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$If the image is still being rendered on the panel and only needs external light to be seen, the LCD and its data path are fine and the illumination source has failed; a working external display proves the GPU and its output stage are also fine. POST beep codes and garbled artifacts point the other way - toward memory, the system board, or the GPU itself - and a laptop that will not power on is a power problem, not a display problem.$q$),

('a1-q-910','aplus1',5,'5.4','multi',2,$q$A projector in a small enclosed cabinet runs normally for about 20 minutes, then shuts itself off with the temperature indicator lit. Select the TWO most appropriate corrective actions.$q$,$q$["Clean or replace the projector's air filter","Relocate the projector so its intake and exhaust vents have unobstructed clearance","Replace the lamp module with a higher-wattage lamp","Lower the display resolution on the source computer","Replace the HDMI cable with a shorter one"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$A thermal shutdown after a consistent warm-up interval is the projector protecting its lamp and optics from overheating, and the two things that restore airflow are a clean filter and unobstructed vents. A higher-wattage lamp is the tempting "fix the lamp" instinct, but it adds heat to an already thermally limited enclosure and is not a supported part; resolution and cable length have no effect on the projector's internal temperature.$q$),

('a1-q-911','aplus1',5,'5.4','multi',3,$q$Select the THREE findings that point to a projector lamp approaching end of life rather than a signal or source problem.$q$,$q$["The projected image is noticeably dimmer than when the unit was new","Colours look washed out and shifted toward yellow","The lamp-hour counter has exceeded the manufacturer's rated lamp life","The projector displays a blue screen with the message 'No Source'","The projected image is trapezoidal, wider at the top than the bottom"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2]'::jsonb,$q$Lamps lose luminous output and colour temperature gradually as they age, so progressive dimming plus a yellow shift, corroborated by the hour counter, is the aging-lamp signature. A "No Source" message is the tempting inclusion, but that message is generated by a healthy lamp illuminating the projector's own menu - it indicates an input or cabling problem; a trapezoidal image is geometry, corrected by repositioning or keystone adjustment.$q$),

('a1-q-912','aplus1',5,'5.4','matching',3,$q$Match each display or projector symptom on the left to its most likely cause on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Monitor displays its own self-test message when the video cable is unplugged, but shows nothing when the cable is connected","Laptop screen is nearly black, yet the image is faintly visible under a bright flashlight","Projector runs fine for about 20 minutes and then shuts itself off","A single dot on the panel stays black no matter what content is displayed"],"right":["Blocked vents or a clogged air filter causing thermal shutdown","A dead pixel with a failed transistor","A faulty video cable or a failed graphics output on the PC","A failed backlight or inverter"]}$q$::jsonb,'[2,3,0,1]'::jsonb,$q$A monitor that produces its own self-test message has a working panel, backlight, and controller, so the fault lies upstream in the cable or the PC's video output. A faintly visible image means the LCD is still rendering but is not being illuminated, which is a backlight or inverter failure. A shutdown after a consistent warm-up period is thermal protection from restricted airflow. A permanently black single dot is a dead pixel - note that it is distinct from a stuck pixel, which is locked on in one colour rather than off.$q$),

('a1-q-913','aplus1',5,'5.4','ordering',2,$q$A user reports no image on an external monitor attached to their desktop. Place the troubleshooting steps in the correct order, from simplest and least invasive to most.$q$,$q$["Test the monitor on a known-good computer to isolate the monitor from the PC","Confirm the monitor is powered on and its power LED is lit","Reseat the video cable at both ends and then try a known-good cable","Set the monitor's input source to match the port the cable is plugged into"]$q$::jsonb,'[1,3,2,0]'::jsonb,$q$Verify power first - an unpowered monitor explains everything and costs nothing to check. Then confirm the input source matches the port in use, since a wrong data source is the single most common cause of a blank display on a working system. Only then disturb the physical connection by reseating and substituting the cable, and finally move the monitor to another machine to isolate whether the panel or the PC is at fault. Swapping the cable first is tempting, but it disturbs a working connection before the two zero-risk checks have been made.$q$),

-- ===== 5.5 Mobile device issues (15) =====

('a1-q-914','aplus1',5,'5.5','mcq',1,$q$A user lands after a flight and reports that their phone shows no signal bars, cannot make calls, and has no mobile data, although Wi-Fi at the airport works. What should the technician check first?$q$,$q$["Whether airplane mode is still enabled on the device","Whether the SIM card has been physically damaged","Whether the mobile data plan has expired","Whether the cellular radio has failed and needs board-level repair"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Airplane mode disables the cellular radio while allowing Wi-Fi to be re-enabled separately, which produces exactly this pattern - working Wi-Fi with no cellular service at all. Toggling it off lets the phone re-register with the carrier. A damaged SIM is the tempting hardware guess, but the timing (immediately after a flight) and the fact that Wi-Fi still works point to a setting, and technicians should always rule out the free software check before opening the SIM tray.$q$),

('a1-q-915','aplus1',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$A user reports that their phone shows "No Service" or a single bar everywhere inside a concrete-and-steel warehouse, but has full bars the moment they step outside. Other users on the same carrier report the same thing in that building. What is the most likely cause and best remedy?$q$,$q$["Signal attenuation from the building structure; enable Wi-Fi calling or install a carrier signal booster or femtocell","Failed cellular radios in all of the affected phones; replace the devices","A corrupted APN configuration on each phone; reset network settings","An expired SIM card; order replacement SIMs from the carrier"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The symptom is location-dependent and affects multiple devices on the same carrier, which rules out any per-device fault - dense concrete and metal attenuate RF, so the fix is to add indoor coverage (Wi-Fi calling, a booster, a femtocell, or a distributed antenna system). Resetting network settings is the tempting standard remedy, but an APN problem would follow the phone outside the building rather than clearing the instant the user walks out the door.$q$),

('a1-q-916','aplus1',5,'5.5','mcq',1,$q$A user complains that their tablet's screen no longer rotates to landscape when they turn the device, in any app. What should the technician check first?$q$,$q$["Whether the rotation/orientation lock has been turned on in the control centre or quick settings","Whether the accelerometer has failed and needs board-level replacement","Whether the tablet needs a full factory reset","Whether the screen protector is interfering with the sensor"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Rotation lock is a single toggle that users enable accidentally, and it suppresses autorotation system-wide, which matches the "in any app" detail exactly. Check and clear it before anything else. A failed accelerometer is the tempting diagnosis because it produces identical symptoms, but it is only a valid conclusion after the lock is confirmed off and a reboot has been attempted - a screen protector cannot affect an internal motion sensor at all.$q$),

('a1-q-917','aplus1',5,'5.5','mcq',3,$q$A technician confirms that rotation lock is off, reboots the phone, and tests several apps that are known to support landscape mode, but the screen still refuses to autorotate. Everything else about the device works normally. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["A failed accelerometer/gyroscope sensor, requiring service or device replacement","A corrupted user profile on the device","An outdated app that has not been updated to support rotation","Insufficient free storage preventing the orientation change"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Once the software toggle is cleared, a reboot has not helped, and multiple rotation-capable apps all fail, the remaining explanation is that the motion sensor itself is no longer reporting orientation - that is a hardware fault requiring service. An app that lacks rotation support is the tempting explanation, but it was explicitly ruled out by testing several apps that do support landscape.$q$),

('a1-q-918','aplus1',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$A phone only charges when the cable is pushed in firmly and held at a particular angle, and the connector feels loose in the socket. A known-good OEM cable and charger behave identically, and the same cable charges another phone without issue. What is the most likely cause, and what is the correct first step?$q$,$q$["A damaged or debris-filled charging port; power the device off and inspect the port, removing lint with a non-metallic tool","A degraded battery; replace the battery","A failed charging IC on the system board; replace the device","A corrupted power-management setting; perform a factory reset"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Because the cable and charger were proven good on another device, the fault is on this phone's side of the connection, and the physical symptoms - loose fit, angle-dependent charging - are textbook signs of a worn, bent, or lint-packed port. Powering down and clearing compacted debris with a plastic or wooden pick is the non-destructive first step before considering a port replacement. A degraded battery is the tempting answer, but a worn battery still accepts a charge reliably at any cable angle - it simply does not hold it for long.$q$),

('a1-q-919','aplus1',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$A user reports that their Android phone suddenly consumed 12 GB of cellular data in three days, shows pop-up ads even when no browser is open, has two apps the user does not recognise, runs hot, and drains its battery by lunchtime. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["Malware installed on the device","The battery has reached the end of its service life","The device needs an operating-system update","The cellular data plan was downgraded by the carrier"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Unfamiliar apps, out-of-browser pop-ups, unexplained data usage, overheating, and rapid drain occurring together is the classic mobile malware profile - a background process is exfiltrating data and serving ads. Respond by uninstalling the unknown apps, updating the OS, scanning with reputable mobile anti-malware, and factory resetting if the symptoms persist. A worn battery is the tempting single-symptom explanation, but it cannot generate pop-up ads, install apps, or consume cellular data.$q$),

('a1-q-920','aplus1',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$A phone was dropped and its glass is cracked across the lower-left corner. The display still shows a bright, correct image everywhere, but taps in the cracked corner register nothing while the rest of the screen responds normally. What is the correct assessment?$q$,$q$["The digitizer (touch layer) is damaged in that area; the screen assembly must be replaced","The touchscreen needs recalibration in the device settings","The LCD panel has failed and the backlight must be replaced","A software glitch is blocking touch input; a factory reset will restore it"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The digitizer is the touch-sensing layer bonded to the cover glass, and physical damage severs its traces in the affected region - the display layer behind it is separate, which is why the image is still perfect. On modern phones the glass, digitizer, and panel are laminated as one unit, so the whole screen assembly is replaced. Recalibrating touch is the tempting option, but calibration adjusts software mapping and cannot restore severed conductors, and a working image rules out an LCD or backlight fault.$q$),

('a1-q-921','aplus1',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$A user complains that their phone takes eight hours to reach a full charge. They charge it overnight from a USB-A port on the back of an older desktop PC. The phone charges to full in under two hours on the manufacturer's wall adapter. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["The USB port supplies far less current than the phone's rated charger","The phone's battery is swollen and must be replaced immediately","The phone's charging port is physically damaged","Malware is throttling the charging circuit"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A legacy USB-A host port typically supplies around 0.5 A, while a modern phone charger delivers several amps under a fast-charge protocol, so charging from the PC is legitimately many times slower. Use the OEM adapter. A swollen battery is the tempting escalation, but swelling presents with visible physical deformation and would not be cured simply by switching to a different power source.$q$),

('a1-q-922','aplus1',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$A two-year-old phone shows battery health at 71% of its original capacity, lasts only three hours of light use, and abruptly powers off at around 30% charge when the camera is opened. The case is flat and undeformed. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["Normal lithium-ion capacity degradation; the battery should be replaced","Malware consuming CPU cycles in the background","A failing charging port limiting the charge accepted","A swollen battery that must be treated as a hazard"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Lithium-ion cells lose capacity and gain internal resistance with cycles and age, and that rising resistance causes the voltage to sag under a heavy load such as the camera, triggering a protective shutdown well above 0% - the reported 71% health figure confirms it. A battery replacement restores normal runtime. Malware is the tempting alternative because it also drains batteries, but it does not lower the reported maximum capacity, and the shutdown occurring specifically under load is a signature of a worn cell.$q$),

('a1-q-923','aplus1',5,'5.5','mcq',1,$q$A user brings in a laptop whose trackpad no longer clicks and which now rocks when placed on a flat desk. The technician notices the bottom case is bowed outward and there is a visible gap along one edge of the chassis. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["The internal battery has swollen and is pressing against the chassis","The chassis was warped by being carried in an overfilled bag","The trackpad cable has come loose from its connector","The bottom-case screws have worked themselves loose"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A swelling lithium-polymer pouch cell expands inside the case, bowing the bottom panel, splitting seams, and pressing up on the trackpad so it can no longer travel - a rocking laptop with a bulging base is the single most recognisable symptom. Loose screws are the tempting mundane explanation, but loose screws produce a rattle or a slightly lifted panel, not a convex bulge that lifts the trackpad off its switch.$q$),

('a1-q-924','aplus1',5,'5.5','mcq',3,$q$A technician confirms a laptop has a visibly swollen lithium-ion battery. What is the correct handling procedure?$q$,$q$["Stop using and stop charging the device, power it off, avoid puncturing or applying pressure, isolate it in a non-flammable container away from combustibles, and dispose of it through a certified hazardous-waste or e-waste recycler","Carefully puncture the pouch with a pin to release the trapped gas, then clamp the cell flat before reinstalling it","Leave the device plugged in and run it until the battery fully discharges, then remove it and place it in the office recycling bin","Place the battery in a freezer overnight to contract the cell, then reinstall it and monitor it for further swelling"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A swollen cell is a fire hazard: it must be de-energised, handled without any mechanical stress, isolated from anything flammable, and taken to a certified hazardous-waste recycler - it must never go in general waste or ordinary recycling. Puncturing is the most dangerous distractor: breaching the pouch exposes flammable electrolyte and lithium compounds to air and can ignite a self-sustaining thermal-runaway fire that ordinary extinguishers cannot smother. Continuing to charge or discharge the pack adds heat to a cell that is already failing, and freezing it causes condensation and further internal damage without relieving the gas.$q$),

('a1-q-925','aplus1',5,'5.5','multi',2,$q$Select the THREE symptoms that most strongly suggest a mobile device is infected with malware rather than simply aging.$q$,$q$["A sudden, unexplained spike in cellular data usage","Pop-up advertisements appearing when no browser is open","Apps the user never installed appearing on the home screen","The device takes slightly longer to charge than it did when new","The screen brightness auto-adjusts in direct sunlight"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2]'::jsonb,$q$Unexplained data consumption indicates something is transmitting in the background, out-of-browser pop-ups indicate an adware payload with system-level display permission, and unrecognised installed apps indicate unauthorised code execution - all three require an active malicious process. Slower charging is the tempting inclusion, but it is a normal consequence of battery wear or a low-current charger, and automatic brightness adjustment is an intended ambient-light-sensor feature.$q$),

('a1-q-926','aplus1',5,'5.5','multi',3,$q$A technician discovers a swollen lithium-ion battery in a tablet. Select the TWO actions the technician must NOT take.$q$,$q$["Puncture or pierce the swollen pouch to vent the trapped gas","Reconnect the charger and continue charging the device until it powers on","Power the device off and stop using it","Place the pack in a non-flammable container away from combustible material","Deliver the pack to a certified e-waste or hazardous-waste recycler"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$Puncturing a swollen cell exposes flammable electrolyte and reactive lithium compounds and can trigger immediate thermal runaway - it is never an acceptable way to relieve the swelling. Continuing to charge a compromised cell adds energy and heat to a pack that is already venting gas, dramatically raising the fire risk. The other three options are the correct procedure: de-energise, isolate safely, and dispose of the pack through certified hazardous-waste channels per local regulation.$q$),

('a1-q-927','aplus1',5,'5.5','multi',2,$q$A phone that is only six months old is draining its battery unusually fast, but its reported battery health is 98% and there is no physical swelling. Select the TWO most likely software or usage causes to investigate.$q$,$q$["Screen brightness at maximum with a long display timeout","A background app with continuous GPS/location access","A worn separation pad in the device","A missing default gateway on the Wi-Fi network","An incorrect display resolution setting"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$With health at 98% and no swelling, the cell is fine, so the drain is coming from consumption: the display is normally the single largest power draw on a phone, and continuous GPS with background refresh keeps power-hungry radios and sensors active. Check the per-app battery usage screen to confirm. A missing default gateway is the tempting network-flavoured distractor, but it causes connectivity failure, not battery drain - although a phone with a genuinely weak cellular signal will drain faster because the radio transmits at higher power.$q$),

('a1-q-928','aplus1',5,'5.5','ordering',2,$q$A phone will not charge from any outlet. Place the troubleshooting steps in the correct order, from least to most invasive.$q$,$q$["Escalate the device for charging-port or battery replacement","Test with a known-good OEM cable and wall adapter in a different, verified outlet","Power the device off and inspect the charging port, clearing lint or debris with a non-metallic tool","Review the battery-health report and inspect the device for swelling or a separating case"]$q$::jsonb,'[1,2,3,0]'::jsonb,$q$Substituting a known-good cable, adapter, and outlet eliminates the most common and cheapest causes without touching the device. Next, inspect and gently clear the port, since compacted pocket lint prevents the connector from seating and is fixed in seconds. Then assess the battery itself for health and swelling, which also determines whether the device is a safety hazard. Only after those checks does hardware replacement become justified - starting with a port or battery swap would risk replacing a perfectly good part.$q$),

-- ===== 5.6 Printer issues (12) =====

('a1-q-929','aplus1',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$A laser printer produces pages on which a faint, repeated copy of the image printed earlier on the same page appears further down. The toner is otherwise dark and adheres properly. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["The imaging drum is not being fully discharged and cleaned between rotations","The fuser is running at too low a temperature","The wrong print driver is installed for the device","The paper tray guides are set for the wrong paper size"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Ghost images occur when residual charge or leftover toner from the previous drum revolution is not removed before the drum is re-exposed, so a faded echo of the earlier content is developed again - the repeat interval matches the drum's circumference. Replacing the drum or cartridge unit resolves it. A cool fuser is the tempting answer, but under-fusing makes the toner smear or flake off when rubbed, and here the toner is stated to adhere properly.$q$),

('a1-q-930','aplus1',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$A shared laser printer frequently pulls three or four sheets through at once, causing jams and wasted paper. The paper is fresh from a newly opened ream and the tray is filled to just below the maximum fill line. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["The separation pad or separation roller is worn and no longer holding back the extra sheets","The pickup roller has lost all traction and cannot grab paper","The fuser assembly has failed","The print driver is set to duplex printing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The separation pad or roller exists specifically to hold back every sheet except the top one; when its friction surface wears smooth, multiple sheets ride through together. Replacing it is a routine maintenance-kit item. A dead pickup roller is the tempting mechanical alternative, but a pickup roller with no traction produces the opposite fault - a "paper out" or no-feed error - rather than pulling too many sheets at once.$q$),

('a1-q-931','aplus1',5,'5.6','multi',2,$q$A laser printer jams at the same point in the paper path several times a day, even with fresh paper and correctly set tray guides. Select the TWO most likely causes.$q$,$q$["Worn or glazed pickup and feed rollers in that section of the path","A torn fragment of paper or a foreign object such as a staple lodged in the path","The print driver is set to the wrong page orientation","The toner cartridge is low","The printer shows as offline in the Windows print queue"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$A jam that recurs at one specific location is a physical obstruction or a mechanical failure at that location - either debris left behind from a previous jam, or rollers whose rubber has hardened and glazed so they no longer feed evenly. Low toner is the tempting inclusion because it is the most familiar printer complaint, but toner level affects print density, not paper transport, and an offline queue prevents printing entirely rather than causing jams.$q$),

('a1-q-932','aplus1',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$A user prints a two-page Word document and the printer ejects nine pages of random characters, stray symbols, and PostScript-like commands. Other users printing to the same device get clean output. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["The wrong or a corrupted print driver is installed on that user's workstation","The toner cartridge is nearly empty","The printer's fuser has failed","The paper type setting does not match the loaded media"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Garbled output means the printer is receiving a data stream it cannot interpret - typically a driver that speaks the wrong page-description language (sending PostScript to a PCL-only device, for example) or one that has become corrupted. Because only one user is affected, the fault is on that workstation: remove and reinstall the correct driver and clear the queue. A failing fuser is the tempting hardware guess, but a fuser problem affects how toner adheres to the page, not what characters are placed on it, and it would affect every user.$q$),

('a1-q-933','aplus1',5,'5.6','mcq',3,$q$Every page from a laser printer carries a smudge that repeats at an identical vertical interval down the sheet. How should the technician identify which component is at fault?$q$,$q$["Measure the distance between the repeating marks and match it to a component's circumference using the repetitive-defect ruler","Reinstall the printer driver on every workstation that uses the device","Replace the fuser assembly first, as it is the most common cause of any repeating mark","Reduce the print density setting until the marks are no longer visible"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A defect that repeats at a fixed interval is caused by damage or contamination on a rotating part, and the interval equals that roller's circumference - the manufacturer's repetitive-defect ruler maps each spacing to a specific component (drum, developer roller, transfer roller, or fuser roller) so the right part is replaced. Replacing the fuser first is the tempting shortcut, but it is a guess that fixes only one of several possible sources and wastes an expensive assembly.$q$),

('a1-q-934','aplus1',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$After a colour laser printer's magenta cartridge is replaced, output has an obvious magenta cast and thin colour fringes appear along the edges of black text. What should the technician do first?$q$,$q$["Run the printer's colour calibration and registration routine from its control panel","Replace the newly installed magenta cartridge with another one","Replace the fuser assembly","Change the paper type setting to heavy stock"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Colour lasers lay down four separate toner planes and must be calibrated so their densities are balanced and registered so the planes align; a new cartridge changes the density characteristics, and misalignment produces coloured fringing around text. Running calibration and registration corrects both symptoms in one step. Swapping in another cartridge is the tempting reaction, but the symptom appeared as a direct consequence of installing a new consumable, which is exactly the condition calibration is designed to correct.$q$),

('a1-q-935','aplus1',5,'5.6','ordering',2,$q$Place the steps for safely clearing a paper jam in a laser printer in the correct order.$q$,$q$["Pull each jammed sheet out in the direction of normal paper travel with steady, even pressure","Power the printer off and allow the fuser to cool before reaching into the paper path","Open the access covers and locate every jammed sheet, including any torn fragments","Close the covers, power the printer back on, and run a test page to confirm the path is clear"]$q$::jsonb,'[1,2,0,3]'::jsonb,$q$Power down and let the fuser cool first - it operates near 200 degrees C and is the primary burn hazard inside a laser printer. Then open the covers and account for every sheet and fragment, because a scrap left behind causes the jam to recur immediately. Pull in the direction of normal travel so toner is not scraped off and the rollers are not driven backwards. Finish with a test page to prove the path is clear before returning the device to the user.$q$),

('a1-q-936','aplus1',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$Print jobs sent to a multifunction printer with a finisher come out correctly printed and in the right order, but they are no longer stapled. Nothing else about the device has changed. What should the technician check first?$q$,$q$["The staple cartridge level and the finisher unit for a staple jam","The imaging drum in the toner cartridge","The 100 m maximum cable length to the printer","The colour calibration settings"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Finishing operations - stapling, hole punching, and booklet folding - happen in a separate finisher unit downstream of the print engine, so pages that print perfectly but arrive unstapled point at an empty staple cartridge, a staple jam, or a finisher option that is no longer selected in the driver. The imaging drum is the tempting printer-side guess, but drum faults change how the page looks, and the printed pages are stated to be correct.$q$),

('a1-q-937','aplus1',5,'5.6','mcq',1,$q$A user sets a Word document to portrait orientation but every copy prints in landscape, cutting off the right side of the page. Other users printing from the same application to the same printer get portrait output. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["Landscape is set as the default orientation in that user's printer driver preferences","The paper tray is loaded with the wrong paper size","The printer's fuser is misaligned","The document contains a corrupted embedded font"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Orientation can be set in the application and in the driver's printing preferences, and a driver default of landscape is applied at print time on that workstation - which is why only this user is affected. Correct the printing defaults in the driver's properties. Wrong paper size in the tray is the tempting alternative, but the tray is shared by every user, so a tray-level problem could not affect one person while others print correctly.$q$),

('a1-q-938','aplus1',5,'5.6','mcq',3,$q$A laser printer produces perfect output on standard 20 lb paper, but when a user prints on heavy cardstock the toner rubs off with a fingernail. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["The media type setting does not match the cardstock, so the fuser is not running at the higher temperature the heavier stock requires","The fuser assembly has failed and must be replaced","The imaging drum is worn out","The toner cartridge is a non-OEM refill"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Fusing depends on the printer applying enough heat and dwell time for the media's thickness; if the driver and control panel still report plain paper, the printer will not slow down or raise fuser temperature for the heavier stock, so the toner never fully melts into the fibres. Setting the media type to cardstock corrects it. A failed fuser is the tempting diagnosis, but a genuinely failed fuser under-fuses every job on every media type, and this printer produces perfect output on plain paper.$q$),

('a1-q-939','aplus1',5,'5.6','multi',2,$q$Pages from a laser printer are uniformly faded across the entire sheet - not streaked, and with no jams. Select the TWO most likely causes.$q$,$q$["The toner cartridge is low and the toner is unevenly distributed","EconoMode/toner-save is enabled in the driver or on the control panel","The page orientation is set to landscape","The printer is showing as offline in the print queue","The finisher's staple cartridge is empty"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$Uniform fading over the whole page means less toner is reaching the paper everywhere, which is caused either by a depleted cartridge (rocking it side to side redistributes the remaining toner as a temporary measure) or by a deliberate toner-saving mode reducing print density. EconoMode is the setting technicians most often overlook, since the printer is behaving exactly as configured. Orientation, queue status, and staple supply have no effect on print density at all.$q$),

('a1-q-940','aplus1',5,'5.6','matching',3,$q$Match each printer symptom on the left to its most likely cause on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["A vertical white streak runs down the same position on every page","Toner smears off the page when the printed sheet is rubbed","Pages of random characters and stray symbols print from one workstation","Three or four sheets feed through the printer at once"],"right":["A worn separation pad or separation roller","An obstruction in the toner path or debris on the laser scanner window","A failed fuser assembly or an incorrect media-type setting","An incorrect or corrupted print driver"]}$q$::jsonb,'[1,2,3,0]'::jsonb,$q$A white streak in a fixed horizontal position means toner is not reaching that column - either the toner path is blocked or the laser cannot reach the drum because the scanner window is dirty. Toner that rubs off was never melted into the paper, which is a fusing failure or a media-type setting that kept the fuser too cool. Garbled characters mean the printer cannot interpret the data stream, which is a driver problem, and it is workstation-specific here. Multiple sheets feeding together means the separation mechanism is no longer holding back the sheets beneath the top one.$q$),

-- ===== 5.7 Wired and wireless network issues (10) =====

('a1-q-941','aplus1',5,'5.7','mcq',2,$q$VoIP calls from a branch office sound choppy and robotic with words clipping in and out, although large file transfers to the same site complete at full speed. A continuous ping to the call server returns round-trip times that swing between 8 ms and 190 ms. What is the problem?$q$,$q$["Jitter - excessive variation in packet delay","Consistently high latency on the WAN link","Insufficient total bandwidth on the circuit","A duplex mismatch on the branch switch uplink"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Wildly varying round-trip times are the definition of jitter, and because voice must be reassembled into a steady real-time stream, irregular arrival empties the jitter buffer and produces choppy, robotic audio. Apply QoS to prioritise voice and place phones on a dedicated voice VLAN. Consistently high latency is the tempting pick, but steady delay causes people to talk over each other rather than making audio break up, and the ping results here show variation rather than a uniformly high figure - full-speed file transfers also rule out a raw bandwidth shortage.$q$),

('a1-q-942','aplus1',5,'5.7','mcq',3,$q$A user reports that their wired connection drops for a few seconds several times an hour. The switch log shows the user's port repeatedly logging link-down followed by link-up, and the port's CRC error counter is climbing steadily. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["A damaged patch cable or a bad termination causing the link to flap","DHCP scope exhaustion on the subnet","A DNS server that is failing to respond","An incorrectly configured default gateway on the workstation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Repeated link up/down transitions - port flapping - plus rising CRC errors are physical-layer evidence: the electrical link is marginal, so it keeps losing and re-establishing sync. Replace the patch cable, then check the jack termination and the NIC. DHCP exhaustion is the tempting answer for intermittent connectivity, but a DHCP failure leaves the link light steady and hands the client an APIPA address rather than causing the switch port itself to cycle.$q$),

('a1-q-943','aplus1',5,'5.7','mcq',2,$q$A workstation on a gigabit switch transfers files at only about 11 MB/s, and its NIC reports a 100 Mbps link. Moving the same workstation to a different port with a different patch cable produces a 1 Gbps link and full-speed transfers. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["A damaged pair or a bad termination in the original cable, which allowed only 100 Mbps negotiation","The ISP is throttling the office connection","The workstation's NIC driver is out of date","The switch does not support gigabit speeds"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$1000BASE-T requires all four pairs, while 100BASE-TX needs only two - so a cable with one broken or miswired pair still links, but negotiates down to 100 Mbps. The fault following the original cable and port proves it is not the workstation. A gigabit-incapable switch is the tempting answer, but the same switch delivered 1 Gbps on another port, and an ISP has no influence on internal LAN transfer speeds.$q$),

('a1-q-944','aplus1',5,'5.7','mcq',2,$q$A workstation has a valid DHCP-assigned address of 192.168.10.55/24 and can reach file servers on 192.168.10.0/24, but it cannot reach anything outside that subnet - not even 8.8.8.8 by IP address. What should the technician check first?$q$,$q$["The default gateway configured on the workstation","The DNS server addresses configured on the workstation","Whether the NIC has failed and needs replacement","Whether the user's account has been locked out"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Local traffic works, so the NIC, cable, switch port, and IP configuration on the local subnet are all fine - what is missing is the router the host uses to reach any other network, which is the default gateway. DNS is the tempting answer for "cannot reach the internet", but the workstation also fails to reach 8.8.8.8 by raw IP address, which requires no name resolution at all and therefore points squarely at routing rather than DNS.$q$),

('a1-q-945','aplus1',5,'5.7','mcq',2,$q$Users complain that a cloud application hosted overseas is slow, while everything on the local network and the company intranet responds instantly. A traceroute shows single-digit millisecond times through the first four hops, then a jump to 260 ms at the fifth hop, which belongs to the ISP's transit provider, and it stays around that figure to the destination. What should the technician do?$q$,$q$["Escalate to the ISP with the traceroute, since the latency is introduced outside the local network","Replace the office core switch, which is adding delay","Increase the DHCP lease duration on the local subnet","Reinstall the cloud application on each affected workstation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Traceroute localises the delay: hops inside the local network respond in single-digit milliseconds, and the step change occurs at a transit provider outside the technician's control, so the evidence should be handed to the ISP. Replacing the core switch is the tempting hardware action, but the local hops are already fast, and swapping healthy equipment would not remove latency introduced hundreds of milliseconds downstream.$q$),

('a1-q-946','aplus1',5,'5.7','mcq',3,$q$A factory floor workstation loses its wired connection for a few seconds whenever a large motor on an adjacent production line starts. The switch shows rising CRC errors on that port, and the Cat5e run is stapled alongside a conduit carrying the motor's power feed. What is the most appropriate fix?$q$,$q$["Reroute the cable away from the power conduit, or replace it with shielded twisted pair or fibre","Assign the workstation a static IP address instead of using DHCP","Change the wireless channel on the nearest access point","Extend the DHCP lease time for that subnet"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Unshielded twisted pair run parallel to a high-current power feed picks up induced electromagnetic interference every time the motor's inrush current spikes, corrupting frames (hence the CRC errors) and dropping the link. Separation, shielding, or fibre - which is immune to EMI - eliminates the coupling. A static IP is the tempting configuration change, but the CRC errors and the exact correlation with motor startup are physical-layer evidence, and no addressing change can prevent electrical noise on a wire.$q$),

('a1-q-947','aplus1',5,'5.7','mcq',2,$q$A technician runs a single Cat6 cable 120 m from the IDF to a new workstation. The link comes up but drops intermittently and the port logs frequent errors. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["The run exceeds the 100 m maximum length for a copper Ethernet channel","Cat6 is not rated for gigabit speeds","The workstation needs a static IP address","The switch port is configured for the wrong VLAN"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Copper Ethernet is specified to a 100 m channel - 90 m of solid-core horizontal cable plus up to 10 m of patch cords - beyond which signal attenuation causes errors and unstable links. Add an intermediate switch or run fibre for that distance. A wrong VLAN is the tempting configuration answer, but a VLAN misassignment puts the host on the wrong network with a stable link, rather than producing physical-layer errors and intermittent drops.$q$),

('a1-q-948','aplus1',5,'5.7','mcq',2,$q$One user at the far end of a warehouse gets 4 Mbps over Wi-Fi and frequent stalls, while users near the access point on the same SSID get well over 200 Mbps. A speed test from a wired port confirms the internet circuit is delivering full speed. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["Weak signal at that distance is forcing the client to negotiate a much slower data rate","The internet circuit is oversubscribed during business hours","The user's device has an outdated web browser","The DNS server is responding slowly to that client"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Wi-Fi clients step down to slower, more robust modulation as signal strength and signal-to-noise ratio fall, so a distant client legitimately achieves a fraction of the throughput of a nearby one - and because it occupies the channel longer per frame, it also degrades everyone else. Add an access point or improve placement. An oversubscribed circuit is the tempting answer, but the wired speed test proved the internet link is fine, and the problem affects only the one distant user.$q$),

('a1-q-949','aplus1',5,'5.7','multi',2,$q$Voice quality on a congested office LAN degrades badly whenever nightly backups overlap with the business day. Select the TWO changes that most directly improve VoIP call quality.$q$,$q$["Enable QoS to classify and prioritise voice traffic ahead of bulk data","Place IP phones on a dedicated voice VLAN separated from data traffic","Set the switch ports to half duplex to reduce collisions","Shorten the DHCP lease time on the data subnet","Disable DNS caching on the workstations"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1]'::jsonb,$q$QoS marks and queues latency-sensitive voice packets ahead of bulk transfers so calls are not stuck behind backup traffic on a congested uplink, and a separate voice VLAN isolates phones from broadcast traffic and makes those QoS policies straightforward to apply consistently. Forcing half duplex is the tempting "reduce collisions" reflex, but it does the opposite on modern switched networks - it halves effective throughput and can create a duplex mismatch that produces errors and further degrades call quality.$q$),

('a1-q-950','aplus1',5,'5.7','multi',2,$q$A user reports that their wired connection drops for a few seconds several times an hour with no pattern. Select the THREE checks that most directly investigate a physical-layer cause.$q$,$q$["Inspect the patch cable and the RJ45 terminations at both ends for damage","Review the switch port for link up/down (flapping) events and rising CRC/FCS error counters","Move the workstation to a different, known-good switch port and observe whether the drops follow it","Flush the DNS resolver cache on the workstation","Increase the DHCP lease duration on the subnet"]$q$::jsonb,'[0,1,2]'::jsonb,$q$Inspecting the cable and terminations, reading the port's flap and error counters, and swapping the workstation to a known-good port are all substitution and evidence-gathering steps that isolate whether the fault follows the cable, the port, or the host - the standard approach for intermittent connectivity. Flushing the DNS cache is the tempting reflex, but DNS problems produce name-resolution failures with a link that never drops, and DHCP lease duration affects address renewal, not link stability.$q$);

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values

-- ===== 5.4 Video, projector and display issues (18) =====

('a1-f-900','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$A monitor reads "No Signal" while the PC is clearly running. What are the first checks?$$,$$Confirm the monitor's input source matches the port the cable is actually plugged into (the single most common cause), then verify the cable is seated at both ends and swap in a known-good cable. On a desktop with a discrete graphics card, also confirm the cable is in the card's port and not the motherboard's onboard video port.$$),
('a1-f-901','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$Why does a flat-panel display look fuzzy, and how is it fixed?$$,$$An LCD or OLED panel has a fixed physical pixel grid, so any resolution other than its native one must be scaled across mismatched pixels, softening every edge uniformly. Set the display to its native resolution. A long or damaged analogue VGA run can also blur the image; raising the sharpness control only masks the effect.$$),
('a1-f-902','aplus1','acronym',5,'5.4',$$LCD$$,$$Liquid Crystal Display - a panel whose liquid crystals modulate light produced by a separate backlight (CCFL on older units, LED on modern ones). Because the light source is separate, a failed backlight leaves the image intact but extremely dim, visible only under a bright external light.$$),
('a1-f-903','aplus1','acronym',5,'5.4',$$OLED$$,$$Organic Light-Emitting Diode - each pixel emits its own light, so no backlight is needed. This yields true blacks and very high contrast, but individual pixels age with use, making OLED panels susceptible to burn-in from long-running static content.$$),
('a1-f-904','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$What is the difference between a dead pixel and a stuck pixel?$$,$$A dead pixel is permanently black on all content, including the pre-boot POST screen, because its transistor has failed - it cannot be revived and is a warranty matter. A stuck pixel is permanently lit in one colour (red, green, or blue) because a subpixel is stuck on, and it can sometimes be recovered with a pixel-cycling utility.$$),
('a1-f-905','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$What causes display burn-in, and how is it prevented?$$,$$Static content left on screen for extended periods (menu bars, dashboards, channel logos) ages those pixels faster than their neighbours on OLED and plasma panels, leaving a permanent faint ghost of the image. Prevent it by rotating content, enabling screen savers and pixel-shift features, and reducing brightness. Burn-in is generally not reversible.$$),
('a1-f-906','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$A laptop screen is nearly black but the image is faintly visible under a flashlight. What failed?$$,$$The backlight or, on older CCFL panels, its inverter. The LCD is still rendering the image and the GPU is still driving it - only the illumination is gone. Confirm by connecting an external monitor: if that displays normally, the GPU and video path are fine and the fault is isolated to the laptop's panel assembly.$$),
('a1-f-907','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$A projected image is dim and washed out with a yellow shift. What is the likely cause?$$,$$The lamp is approaching end of life - lamps lose luminous output and drift in colour temperature as they age. Check the lamp-hour counter against the rated life, and also rule out eco/low-power mode, a dirty lens, and a clogged filter. Replace the lamp and reset the counter so the next end-of-life warning is accurate.$$),
('a1-f-908','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$A projector shuts itself off after 15-30 minutes with the temperature indicator lit. What is wrong?$$,$$Thermal shutdown from restricted airflow - a clogged air filter, blocked or obstructed intake/exhaust vents, an enclosed cabinet with no clearance, or a failing cooling fan. Clean or replace the filter, ensure the manufacturer's clearance around all vents, and verify the fan spins. Never fit a higher-wattage lamp to compensate.$$),
('a1-f-909','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$A projector fan runs but nothing is projected and the lamp LED is red. What should you do?$$,$$The lamp has burned out. Power down, let the unit cool fully, and install the correct OEM lamp module, handling it only by the housing - skin oils on the glass create hot spots and cause premature failure. Reset the lamp-hour counter after replacement.$$),
('a1-f-910','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$Video works over HDMI to a display or projector but there is no sound. What is the fix?$$,$$HDMI and DisplayPort carry embedded audio, but the operating system does not always switch to the new device automatically - set the display or projector as the default playback device in sound settings. Also check per-application volume and whether the display's own speakers are muted or turned down. (VGA and DVI-D are video only and genuinely do need a separate audio cable.)$$),
('a1-f-911','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$What causes a flashing or flickering screen?$$,$$A refresh rate set too low or to an unsupported mode, a loose or failing video cable, a failing backlight or its power circuit, or electrical interference. Start with the cheapest checks: set a supported refresh rate (60 Hz or higher), then reseat and substitute the cable before suspecting the panel.$$),
('a1-f-912','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$The whole screen has a strong colour tint. How do you tell hardware from software?$$,$$If the tint is present on the BIOS/POST screen before the OS loads, or it follows the cable when cables are swapped, it is physical - a damaged conductor or a bent/broken connector pin dropping one colour channel, classic on VGA and DVI. If it only appears after the OS loads, suspect the ICC colour profile, the driver, or the monitor's colour-temperature preset.$$),
('a1-f-913','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$A 4K 60 Hz display shows sparkles, black flashes, and dropouts, but works at 1080p. Why?$$,$$The link lacks the bandwidth for the higher mode. 4K at 60 Hz needs roughly 18 Gbps, which an uncertified or overly long passive HDMI cable cannot sustain, so the signal repeatedly loses sync. Use a certified Premium High Speed HDMI cable, a DisplayPort cable, or an active/optical cable for long runs.$$),
('a1-f-914','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$A projected image is trapezoidal - wider at the top than the bottom. What is this and how is it fixed?$$,$$Keystone distortion, caused by the projector not being perpendicular to the screen. Correct it physically first by squaring the projector to the screen and using the lens shift or adjustable feet, then apply keystone correction as a last resort - digital keystone correction resamples the image and slightly reduces sharpness.$$),
('a1-f-915','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$How do you isolate a blank display to the monitor versus the PC?$$,$$Disconnect the video cable from the monitor. If the monitor then shows its own self-test pattern or on-screen menu, its panel, backlight, and controller are all working and the fault is upstream in the cable or the PC's video output. If the monitor stays completely dark with no OSD at all, the monitor itself is suspect.$$),
('a1-f-916','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$An image is stretched, squashed, or surrounded by black bars. What causes this?$$,$$An aspect-ratio or scaling mismatch - the source resolution's shape does not match the panel's, and the display or GPU is set to stretch to fill rather than preserve the aspect ratio. Set the correct native resolution and choose the aspect-preserving scaling mode in the display's OSD or the GPU control panel. On TVs, also check for an overscan setting cutting off the edges.$$),
('a1-f-917','aplus1','core',5,'5.4',$$A docked laptop shows nothing on the second monitor. What are the checks?$$,$$Use Win+P to confirm the projection mode is Extend or Duplicate rather than PC screen only, verify the monitor's input source matches the dock port in use, confirm the dock is fully seated and powered, and check that the display appears in Display settings (use Detect if it does not). Also verify the dock supports the number and resolution of displays being attached.$$),

-- ===== 5.5 Mobile device issues (18) =====

('a1-f-918','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$A phone has no cellular signal or data. What is the triage sequence?$$,$$Confirm airplane mode is off and cellular data is enabled, toggle airplane mode on and off to force re-registration with the carrier, reboot, verify the SIM is seated and undamaged, check the APN settings, and test outdoors to rule out building attenuation. Escalate to carrier or hardware only after those checks fail.$$),
('a1-f-919','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$A mobile device shows Wi-Fi connected but has no internet access. What do you check?$$,$$Look for an unaccepted captive portal login page, forget the network and rejoin so a fresh DHCP lease and gateway are obtained, verify the device received a valid address and gateway, and test another device on the same SSID to determine whether the fault is the device or the access point.$$),
('a1-f-920','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$A mobile screen will not autorotate. In what order do you troubleshoot it?$$,$$First confirm rotation/orientation lock is off in the control centre or quick settings - the most common cause by far. Then test an app known to support landscape, since not every app rotates. Then reboot the device. If rotation still fails everywhere after all three, the accelerometer/gyroscope has failed and the device needs service.$$),
('a1-f-921','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$What are the signs of a physically damaged charging port, and what is the first fix?$$,$$Charging only at certain cable angles, a loose or wobbly connector fit, intermittent charging, and visibly bent, corroded, or recessed pins. First rule out the cable and adapter with known-good OEM parts, then power the device off and clear compacted lint or debris from the port with a plastic or wooden pick - never a metal tool. Port replacement is the last step.$$),
('a1-f-922','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$What symptoms suggest mobile malware, and how do you respond?$$,$$Unexplained spikes in data usage, pop-up ads outside a browser, apps the user never installed, rapid battery drain, overheating, sluggish performance, and unexpected premium charges. Respond by updating the OS and apps, uninstalling unrecognised apps, revoking suspicious permissions, scanning with reputable mobile anti-malware, and factory resetting from a known-clean backup if symptoms persist.$$),
('a1-f-923','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$What is the digitizer, and how does a cracked screen affect it?$$,$$The digitizer is the touch-sensing layer bonded to the cover glass, separate from the display panel behind it. Physical cracks can sever its traces so touch stops working in one region while the image remains perfect; conversely the panel can fail while touch still works. Because modern phones laminate glass, digitizer, and panel together, the whole screen assembly is replaced.$$),
('a1-f-924','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$What causes improper or very slow charging on a mobile device?$$,$$Charging from a low-current source such as a legacy USB-A host port (around 0.5 A) instead of the rated wall adapter, a damaged or non-compliant cable, an aftermarket charger that does not support the device's fast-charge protocol, a lint-filled or damaged port, or charging in extreme heat where the device throttles for safety. Test with the OEM adapter and cable first.$$),
('a1-f-925','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$What indicates poor battery health rather than a software problem?$$,$$Runtime far shorter than when new, abrupt shutdowns at moderate charge levels (especially under heavy load such as the camera), the device running hot, and a battery-health or cycle-count report showing capacity well below 100%. Rising internal resistance in an aged cell makes voltage sag under load, triggering protective shutdown. The remedy is battery replacement.$$),
('a1-f-926','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$What causes a lithium-ion battery to swell, and how do you recognise it?$$,$$Gas builds up inside the pouch cell as the electrolyte breaks down from age, heat, overcharging, or physical damage. Recognise it by a bulging or bowed bottom case, a lifting or separating display, a laptop that rocks on a flat desk, a trackpad that no longer clicks, or gaps opening along chassis seams.$$),
('a1-f-927','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$What is the correct safe handling procedure for a swollen lithium-ion battery?$$,$$Stop using the device and stop charging it immediately, power it off, and do not puncture, crush, bend, or apply pressure to the pack. Do not freeze it or submerge it. Move it, without mechanical stress, to a cool, dry, non-flammable container away from combustible material, and dispose of it through a certified e-waste or hazardous-waste recycler in accordance with local regulation and the manufacturer's SDS. Never place it in general waste or ordinary recycling.$$),
('a1-f-928','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$Why must you never puncture a swollen battery to release the gas?$$,$$Lithium-ion cells contain a flammable electrolyte and reactive lithium compounds. Breaching the pouch exposes them to air and can trigger thermal runaway - a self-sustaining fire that generates its own oxidiser and cannot be smothered by conventional means, while releasing toxic fumes. Never vent, deflate, clamp, or flatten a swollen pack; isolate it and hand it to a certified recycler.$$),
('a1-f-929','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$A nearly new phone drains fast but reports 98% battery health. What causes this?$$,$$Consumption rather than cell wear: maximum screen brightness with a long display timeout, background location/GPS and app refresh, a runaway or poorly behaved app, excessive push sync, or a persistently weak cellular signal that forces the radio to transmit at full power. Check the per-app battery usage screen to identify the offender.$$),
('a1-f-930','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$What causes a mobile device to overheat, and what should you do?$$,$$Heavy load while charging (gaming, navigation, video), direct sunlight, a thick insulating case, a faulty charger or cable, or malware running a background process. Remove the case, stop charging, close demanding apps, and let it cool away from heat sources. Persistent overheating combined with any swelling means the battery must be replaced and the device treated as a hazard.$$),
('a1-f-931','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$A mobile device will not charge at all. What is the step order?$$,$$Test a known-good OEM cable and wall adapter in a verified outlet, then power off and inspect and clean the charging port with a non-metallic tool, then check the battery-health report and inspect for swelling or a separating case, and only then escalate for port or battery replacement. Working from cheapest and least invasive outward avoids replacing good parts.$$),
('a1-f-932','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$What is the difference between no connectivity and poor connectivity on a mobile device?$$,$$No connectivity means the device never associates or registers at all - airplane mode on, radio disabled, a bad or missing SIM, or being completely out of range. Poor connectivity means the device is connected but the link is weak or slow - distance, building attenuation, congestion, or interference. The two have different fixes, so establish which one you are looking at first.$$),
('a1-f-933','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$After a drop, how do you tell a failed display from a failed digitizer?$$,$$If the image is bright and correct but touch is dead in the damaged area, the digitizer's traces are severed. If the screen is black or shows artifacts while the device still responds to touch, rings, and plays audio, the display panel or its connector failed. Either way, laminated modern assemblies are replaced as a single unit.$$),
('a1-f-934','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$A mobile device will not pair over Bluetooth. What do you check?$$,$$Confirm Bluetooth is enabled and the accessory is actually in pairing mode, that it is within range, and that it is not already connected to another host (many accessories only hold one active link). Remove the stale pairing entry on both devices and pair again, and check whether the device has hit its maximum number of paired devices.$$),
('a1-f-935','aplus1','core',5,'5.5',$$What is a liquid damage indicator, and what should you do with a wet device?$$,$$Phones and tablets contain LDI/LCI stickers in the SIM tray or port that turn from white to pink or red on contact with liquid, and a tripped indicator normally voids the warranty. Do not charge or power on a wet device - applying voltage to a wet board causes short circuits and corrosion. Power it off, dry it thoroughly, and only then attempt to power it up.$$),

-- ===== 5.6 Printer issues (15) =====

('a1-f-936','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$A laser printer produces ghost images - a faint repeat of earlier content further down the page. Why?$$,$$Residual charge or leftover toner is not being cleared from the imaging drum before it is re-exposed, so a faded echo of the previous revolution is developed again. It is caused by a worn cleaning blade or an end-of-life drum, and the repeat interval matches the drum's circumference. Replace the drum or cartridge unit.$$),
('a1-f-937','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$How do you identify which component causes a mark that repeats at a fixed interval down the page?$$,$$Measure the spacing between the repeating defects and match it to the circumference of a rotating component using the manufacturer's repetitive-defect ruler - each interval maps to a specific part (imaging drum, developer roller, transfer roller, or fuser roller). This identifies the exact part to replace instead of guessing at the most expensive assembly.$$),
('a1-f-938','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$A printer outputs pages of random characters and stray symbols. What is wrong?$$,$$The printer is receiving data it cannot interpret - almost always the wrong or a corrupted print driver, such as sending PostScript to a PCL-only device. Remove and reinstall the correct driver for the exact model, clear the print queue and restart the spooler. A failing cable or interface can also corrupt the data stream. If only one workstation is affected, the fault is on that workstation.$$),
('a1-f-939','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$A printer feeds several sheets at once (multipage misfeed). What causes it?$$,$$A worn separation pad or separation roller that no longer holds back the sheets under the top one, an overfilled tray, damp or curled paper, or static-stuck stock. Fan the stack before loading, keep the tray below the fill line, store paper in a dry place, and replace the separation pad - it is a standard maintenance-kit item.$$),
('a1-f-940','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$A printer jams repeatedly at the same point in the path. What are the likely causes?$$,$$A torn paper fragment or foreign object (staple, paper clip) left in that section, worn or glazed pickup and feed rollers, damp or wrong-weight media, or tray guides not set to the loaded paper size. A jam that recurs at one specific location is physical and local to that location, not a driver or queue problem.$$),
('a1-f-941','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$What is the correct procedure for clearing a paper jam in a laser printer?$$,$$Power the printer off and let the fuser cool first - it runs near 200 degrees C and is the main burn hazard. Open the covers, locate every jammed sheet including torn fragments, and pull each out in the direction of normal paper travel with steady even pressure so toner is not scraped off and the rollers are not driven backwards. Close up, power on, and print a test page to confirm the path is clear.$$),
('a1-f-942','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$Toner rubs off on cardstock but plain paper prints perfectly. What is the cause?$$,$$The media-type setting does not match the stock, so the printer never raises the fuser temperature or slows the paper for the heavier media and the toner is not fully melted into the fibres. Set the correct paper type in both the driver and the printer's control panel. A genuinely failed fuser under-fuses every job on every media type.$$),
('a1-f-943','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$A colour laser has a colour cast or coloured fringes around text. What is the fix?$$,$$Run the printer's colour calibration and registration routine from its control panel - calibration rebalances the density of each toner plane (commonly needed after a cartridge change) and registration realigns the four planes so they overlay precisely, removing the fringing. Also verify each cartridge's level and check for a failing individual imaging unit.$$),
('a1-f-944','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$A printer makes a grinding or clunking noise. What should you do?$$,$$Stop and power the printer off before running more jobs - grinding indicates worn gears, a failing roller or bushing, or a foreign object such as a staple or paper fragment in the paper path, and continuing can destroy the drive train. Open the covers and inspect the path and gear train, remove any debris, and replace the worn assembly.$$),
('a1-f-945','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$Pages print correctly but are not stapled, punched, or collated. What do you check?$$,$$Finishing happens in a separate finisher unit downstream of the print engine, so check the finisher for a jam, an empty staple cartridge, or a full hole-punch waste bin. Also confirm the finishing option was actually requested in the driver or application and that the driver reports the finisher as an installed accessory.$$),
('a1-f-946','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$Documents print in the wrong orientation. Where do you look?$$,$$Orientation can be set in the application and in the printer driver's printing preferences, and the driver's default is applied at print time - a driver defaulting to landscape overrides the user's expectation on that workstation only. Correct the printing defaults in the driver's properties, and confirm the paper-size setting and tray guides match the loaded stock.$$),
('a1-f-947','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$A laser printer produces uniformly faded output across the whole page. What causes it?$$,$$Low toner (rock the cartridge gently side to side to redistribute the remainder as a temporary measure), EconoMode or toner-save enabled in the driver or on the panel, a print-density setting turned down, or a failing transfer roller or high-voltage power supply. EconoMode is the most commonly overlooked cause because the printer is behaving exactly as configured.$$),
('a1-f-948','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$A white streak runs down the same position on every printed page. What causes it?$$,$$Toner is not reaching that column - either an obstruction in the toner path inside the cartridge, or debris or toner dust on the laser scanner mirror/window blocking the beam. Clean the scanner window with the printer's supplied tool or a lint-free swab, and replace the cartridge if the streak follows the cartridge to another printer.$$),
('a1-f-949','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$How do you isolate a printing fault to the printer versus the PC or network?$$,$$Print a test or configuration page from the printer's own control panel. If it prints cleanly, the print engine, consumables, and paper path are all healthy and the fault lies in the driver, the print queue or spooler, the application, or the network connection. If the self-generated page shows the same defect, the problem is inside the printer.$$),
('a1-f-950','aplus1','core',5,'5.6',$$A printer outputs completely blank pages. What are the likely causes?$$,$$On a laser: the sealing tape was left on a new toner cartridge, the toner is empty, or the transfer roller or high-voltage supply has failed so no toner is attracted to the page. On an inkjet: empty cartridges or dried, clogged printhead nozzles - run the printer's cleaning cycle and print a nozzle-check pattern. Also confirm the correct driver is selected and the job is not printing to a different device.$$),

-- ===== 5.7 Wired and wireless network issues (12) =====

('a1-f-951','aplus1','acronym',5,'5.7',$$VoIP$$,$$Voice over Internet Protocol - carrying telephone calls as IP packets across a data network. Because voice is real-time, VoIP is far more sensitive to latency, jitter, and packet loss than to raw bandwidth, which is why QoS and a dedicated voice VLAN matter more than adding capacity.$$),
('a1-f-952','aplus1','core',5,'5.7',$$What is jitter, and what does it sound like on a VoIP call?$$,$$Jitter is variation in packet arrival delay. Voice must be reassembled into a steady stream, so irregular arrival empties the jitter buffer and produces choppy, robotic, clipping audio even when average bandwidth is plentiful. Detect it with a continuous ping showing widely varying round-trip times; fix it with QoS, a voice VLAN, and by relieving congestion on the link.$$),
('a1-f-953','aplus1','core',5,'5.7',$$How do latency, jitter, and packet loss present differently?$$,$$High latency is a consistent delay - callers talk over each other and applications feel sluggish, but audio is clear. Jitter is variable delay - audio becomes choppy, robotic, or garbled. Packet loss drops content outright - words disappear, video freezes, and file transfers stall on retransmission. Matching the symptom to the metric points you at the right fix.$$),
('a1-f-954','aplus1','core',5,'5.7',$$What is port flapping, what causes it, and how do you fix it?$$,$$A switch port repeatedly logging link-down then link-up, so users lose connectivity for seconds at a time. It is caused by a damaged or marginal patch cable, a bad termination or wall jack, a failing NIC or transceiver, a loose connector, or a speed/duplex mismatch. Substitute in order: patch cable, then switch port, then NIC, watching whether the flapping follows the swapped component.$$),
('a1-f-955','aplus1','core',5,'5.7',$$A gigabit-capable link negotiates only 100 Mbps. What causes this?$$,$$1000BASE-T requires all four pairs while 100BASE-TX needs only two, so a cable with one broken, shorted, or miswired pair still links but drops to 100 Mbps. Other causes are a bad termination, a 10/100-only switch port or NIC, or a manually forced speed setting. Re-terminate or replace the cable and confirm with a cable tester or certifier.$$),
('a1-f-956','aplus1','core',5,'5.7',$$What is the 100 m rule, and what happens when you exceed it?$$,$$A copper Ethernet channel is specified to a maximum of 100 m - 90 m of solid-core horizontal cabling plus up to 10 m of stranded patch cords at both ends. Beyond that, attenuation causes errors, intermittent drops, and failed or degraded negotiation. Insert an intermediate switch, or use fibre for the long run.$$),
('a1-f-957','aplus1','core',5,'5.7',$$How does external interference affect a copper network run?$$,$$Unshielded twisted pair routed near fluorescent ballasts, motors, elevator machinery, or power conduits picks up induced electromagnetic interference, showing as CRC/FCS errors, retransmissions, and drops that correlate with the noise source switching on. Reroute the cable away from the source, maintain separation from power feeds, or use shielded twisted pair or fibre, which is immune to EMI.$$),
('a1-f-958','aplus1','core',5,'5.7',$$A host has a valid IP but only limited connectivity. How do you narrow it down?$$,$$If it reaches devices on its own subnet but nothing beyond, suspect a missing or wrong default gateway, or a subnet mask that misclassifies remote addresses as local. If it reaches remote hosts by IP address but not by name, the fault is DNS. Pinging a public IP such as 8.8.8.8 separates the two, because that test needs no name resolution.$$),
('a1-f-959','aplus1','core',5,'5.7',$$How do you tell a LAN problem from an ISP or WAN problem?$$,$$Run tracert/traceroute to the destination and find where the round-trip times jump or where responses stop. If the first slow or failing hop is inside your network, the fault is local. If the local hops answer in single-digit milliseconds and the delay appears at the ISP's edge or a transit provider, escalate to the provider and supply the trace as evidence.$$),
('a1-f-960','aplus1','core',5,'5.7',$$Why is one distant Wi-Fi user slow when nearby users on the same SSID are fast?$$,$$As signal strength and signal-to-noise ratio fall, the client negotiates a slower, more robust modulation rate, so throughput drops sharply with distance and obstructions. Worse, the slow client occupies the channel longer per frame, consuming airtime and degrading performance for everyone on that access point. Fix it with better AP placement, an additional AP, or by moving the client closer.$$),
('a1-f-961','aplus1','core',5,'5.7',$$What does QoS do for voice traffic, and what is it usually paired with?$$,$$QoS classifies, marks, and queues latency-sensitive traffic such as voice and video ahead of bulk data, so calls are not stuck behind backups and large downloads on a congested uplink. It is normally paired with a dedicated voice VLAN, which isolates phones from data broadcast traffic and makes the QoS markings straightforward to apply and trust consistently.$$),
('a1-f-962','aplus1','core',5,'5.7',$$What is the checklist for intermittent wired connectivity?$$,$$Check the switch port for link up/down (flapping) entries and rising CRC/FCS error counters, inspect and substitute the patch cable and check the terminations, move the host to a known-good port to see whether the fault follows it, verify the run is within 100 m and not routed alongside EMI sources, and finally test the NIC. Errors that follow the cable confirm a physical-layer fault rather than a configuration problem.$$);
